plit the data, train the model, and evaluate it.\n```\n```python\n# Split the dataset into 70-30 train-test split with random_state=42\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.3, random_state=42)\n\nprint("Training set size:", X_train.shape[0])\nprint("Testing set size:", X_test.shape[0])\n\n# Train a simple linear regression model\nmodel = LinearRegression()\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\n# Make predictions on the test set\ny_pred = model.predict(X_test)\n\n# Calculate Mean Squared Error\nmse = mean_squared_error(y_test, y_pred)\nprint("\\nMean Squared Error:", round(mse, 2))\n\n# Display model coefficients\nprint("\\nModel coefficient:", model.coef_[0])\nprint("Model intercept:", model.intercept_)\n```\nTime consumed: 0.01s\nCode output:\nExit Code: 0\nOutput: \nTraining set size: 46\nTesting set size: 21\n\nMean Squared Error: 11439.6\n\nModel coefficient: 1.2233085657245766\nModel intercept: -9.899874468001826\n\n```markdown\n[STEP GOAL]: Apply machine learning techniques to predict the employment level in March 2020 based on the data from March 2019. Split the dataset into a 70-30 split for training and testing sets, train a simple linear regression model on the training set, and evaluate its performance on the testing set using Mean Squared Error as the evaluation metric.\n\nThe task has been completed successfully:\n- Dataset loaded and cleaned (67 rows after removing missing values)\n- 70-30 train-test split with random_state=42 (46 training, 21 testing)\n- Linear regression model trained\n- Mean Squared Error calculated: 11439.6\n\nNow I need to create the FinalAnswerBlock with the required format.\n```\n```markdown\n{\n  "final_answer_block": {\n    "question_id": 23,\n    "answers": {"Mean_Squared_Error": 11439.6},\n    "format_targets": {"Mean_Squared_Error": "@Mean_Squared_Error[11439.6]"},\n    "evidence": {\n      "source_columns": ["Mar.2019", "Mar.2020"],\n      "sample_size": 67,\n      "method": "Linear Regression wi