rosity\', \'Dystopia Residual\']\n\n```markdown\n# Debugging Summary\nThe debugging process successfully identified and resolved the issue with correlation calculation. The main problem was that the DataFrame contained string columns (\'Country\', \'Region\') which caused a ValueError when trying to convert to float for correlation calculation. The solution was to use the `numeric_only=True` parameter in the `corr()` method to ensure only numeric columns were included in the correlation calculation.\n\nKey findings:\n- The variable with the strongest correlation with Happiness Score is "Happiness Rank"\n- The correlation coefficient is -0.9921 (very strong negative correlation)\n- This makes sense as Happiness Rank is inversely related to Happiness Score (rank 1 = highest score)\n\nThe final answer has been computed and formatted according to the required output format.\n```\n```markdown\n{\n  "final_answer_block": {\n    "question_id": 117,\n    "answers": {\n      "strongest_correlation_variable": "Happiness Rank",\n      "correlation_type": "negative"\n    },\n    "format_targets": {\n      "strongest_correlation_variable": "@strongest_correlation_variable[Happiness Rank]",\n      "correlation_type": "@correlation_type[negative]"\n    },\n    "evidence": {\n      "source_columns": ["Happiness Score", "Happiness Rank", "Standard Error", "Economy (GDP per Capita)", "Family", "Health (Life Expectancy)", "Freedom", "Trust (Government Corruption)", "Generosity", "Dystopia Residual"],\n      "sample_size": 158,\n      "method": "Pearson correlation coefficient calculated between Happiness Score and all other numerical variables using numeric_only=True",\n      "oracle_evidence": {\n        "strongest_variable": "Happiness Rank",\n        "correlation_value": -0.9921,\n        "correlation_sign": "negative"\n      }\n    },\n    "verified": true,\n    "validator_report_id": null\n  }\n}\n```\n```markdown\nNow I need to calculate the Pearson correlation coefficients between "Happiness Score" and all other numerical variables to identify the strongest correlation.\n```\n```markdown\n# Debugging Summary\n\nThe debugging process successfully identified and resolved the issue with correlation calculation. The main problem was that `Series.corr()` expects another Series, not a DataFrame. The solution was to use `DataFrame.corr()` to compute the full correlation matrix and then extract the "Happiness Score" row.\n\nKey findings:\n- The variable with the strongest correlation with Happiness Score is "Happiness Rank"\n- The correlation coefficient is -0.9921 (very strong negative correlation)\n- This makes sense as Happiness Rank is inversely related to Happiness Score (rank 1 = highest score)\n\nAll correlations calculated:\n| Variable | Correlation |\n|----------|-------------|\n| Happiness Rank | -0.9921 |\n| Standard Error | -0.1773 |\n| Economy (GDP per Capita) | 0.7810 |\n| Family | 0.7406 |\n| Health (Life Expectancy) | 0.7242 |\n| Freedom | 0.5682 |\n| Trust (Government Corruption) | 0.3952 |\n| Generosity | 0.1803 |\n| Dystopia Residual | 0.5305 |\n```\n```markdown\n{\n  "final_answer_block": {\n    "question_id": 117,\n    "answers": {\n      "strongest_correlation_variable": "Happiness Rank",\n      "correlation_type": "negative"\n    },\n    "format_targets": {\n      "strongest_correlation_variable": "@strongest_correlation_variable[Happiness Rank]",\n      "correlation_type": "@correlation_type[negative]"\n    },\n    "evidence": {\n      "source_columns": ["Happiness Score", "Happiness Rank", "Standard Error", "Economy (GDP per Capita)", "Family", "Health (Life Expectancy)", "Freedom", "Trust (Government Corruption)", "Generosity", "Dystopia Residual"],\n      "sample_size": 158,\n      "method": "Pearson correlation coefficient calculated between Happiness Score and all other numerical variables using DataFrame.corr()",\n      "oracle_evidence": {\n        "strongest_variable": "Happiness Rank",\n        "correlation_value"