          -0.177254\nName: Happiness Score, dtype: float64\n\nTime consumed: 0.01s\nCode output:\nExit Code: 0\nOutput: \n\nStrongest Correlation Variable: Happiness Rank\nCorrelation Coefficient: -0.9921\nCorrelation Type: negative\n\n```markdown\n[STEP GOAL]: Load the 2015.csv dataset and calculate Pearson correlation coefficients between "Happiness Score" and all other numerical variables to identify the strongest correlation.\n```\n```markdown\nThe correlation analysis is complete. I have successfully:\n1. Loaded the 2015.csv dataset (158 rows, 12 columns)\n2. Calculated Pearson correlation coefficients between "Happiness Score" and all other numerical variables\n3. Identified that "Happiness Rank" has the strongest correlation with a coefficient of -0.9921\n4. Determined the correlation type is negative\n\nThe results show:\n- **Strongest Correlation Variable**: Happiness Rank (|r| = 0.9921)\n- **Correlation Type**: Negative (r = -0.9921)\n\nThis makes logical sense because Happiness Rank is inversely related to Happiness Score - countries with rank 1 have the highest happiness scores, while countries with rank 158 have the lowest scores.\n```\n```markdown\n{\n  "final_answer_block": {\n    "question_id": 117,\n    "answers": {\n      "strongest_correlation_variable": "Happiness Rank",\n      "correlation_type": "negative"\n    },\n    "format_targets": {\n      "strongest_correlation_variable": "@strongest_correlation_variable[Happiness Rank]",\n      "correlation_type": "@correlation_type[negative]"\n    },\n    "evidence": {\n      "source_columns": ["Happiness Score", "Happiness Rank", "Standard Error", "Economy (GDP per Capita)", "Family", "Health (Life Expectancy)", "Freedom", "Trust (Government Corruption)", "Generosity", "Dystopia Residual"],\n      "sample_size": 158,\n      "method": "Pearson correlation coefficient calculated between Happiness Score and all other numerical variables using DataFrame.corr()",\n      "oracle_evidence": {\n        "strongest_variable": "Happiness Rank",\n        "correlation_value": -0.9921,\n        "correlation_sign": "negative"\n      }\n    },\n    "verified": true,\n    "validator_report_id": null\n  }\n}\n```\n```markdown\n<end_step>\n```\n```markdown\n# Analysis Complete: Correlation with Happiness Scores\n\n## Summary of Findings\n\nI have successfully analyzed the 2015.csv dataset to determine which variable has the strongest correlation with happiness scores among countries.\n\n### Methodology\n- **Data Source**: 2015.csv (158 countries, 12 columns)\n- **Statistical Method**: Pearson correlation coefficient (r)\n- **Target Variable**: Happiness Score\n- **Comparison Variables**: All other numerical columns in the dataset\n\n### Correlation Results (sorted by absolute value)\n\n| Variable | Correlation Coefficient (r) | Strength |\n|----------|----------------------------|----------|\n| **Happiness Rank** | **-0.9921** | **Very Strong** |\n| Economy (GDP per Capita) | 0.7810 | Strong |\n| Family | 0.7406 | Strong |\n| Health (Life Expectancy) | 0.7242 | Strong |\n| Freedom | 0.5682 | Moderate |\n| Dystopia Residual | 0.5305 | Moderate |\n| Trust (Government Corruption) | 0.3952 | Weak-Moderate |\n| Generosity | 0.1803 | Weak |\n| Standard Error | -0.1773 | Weak |\n\n### Key Findings\n\n1. **Strongest Correlation Variable**: **Happiness Rank**\n   - Correlation coefficient: **-0.9921**\n   - This is an extremely strong negative correlation\n\n2. **Correlation Type**: **Negative**\n   - As the Happiness Rank increases (worse rank), the Happiness Score decreases\n   - This makes logical sense since rank 1 represents the happiest country with the highest score\n\n3. **Interpretation**: The near-perfect negative correlation (-0.9921) between Happiness Rank and Happiness Score is expected because these two variables are mathematically related - the rank is derived directly from the happiness score. Countries with higher happiness scores receive lower (better) ranks.\n\n### Final