 which missed countries that use multiple vaccines (e.g., "Moderna, Oxford/AstraZeneca, Pfizer/BioNTech"). I need to check if the vaccine column contains any of the target vaccines as substrings.\n```\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\n# Load the data\ndf = pd.read_csv(\'./input/country_vaccinations.csv\')\n\n# Define the vaccines to consider\ntarget_vaccines = [\'Pfizer/BioNTech\', \'Moderna\', \'Oxford/AstraZeneca\', \'Johnson&Johnson/Janssen\']\n\n# Filter for rows where the vaccines column contains any of the target vaccines\ndef contains_target_vaccine(vaccine_str):\n    if pd.isna(vaccine_str):\n        return False\n    for vaccine in target_vaccines:\n        if vaccine in vaccine_str:\n            return True\n    return False\n\ndf_filtered = df[df[\'vaccines\'].apply(contains_target_vaccine)].copy()\n\n# Remove rows with null values in total_vaccinations_per_hundred\ndf_clean = df_filtered.dropna(subset=[\'total_vaccinations_per_hundred\'])\n\nprint(f"Shape after filtering: {df_clean.shape}")\nprint(f"\\nVaccine distribution after filtering:")\nprint(df_clean[\'vaccines\'].value_counts())\n```\n```markdown\nNow I need to group by the primary vaccine type. Since some countries use multiple vaccines, I\'ll need to determine how to handle this. Let me check the data more carefully.\n```\n```python\n# Check unique vaccine combinations\nprint("\\nUnique vaccine combinations:")\nprint(df_clean[\'vaccines\'].unique()[:20])\n\n# For ANOVA, I need to assign each country to a single vaccine group\n# Strategy: Use the first vaccine from the target list that appears in the string\ndef get_primary_vaccine(vaccine_str):\n    if pd.isna(vaccine_str):\n        return None\n    for vaccine in target_vaccines:\n        if vaccine in vaccine_str:\n            return vaccine\n    return None\n\ndf_clean[\'primary_vaccine\'] = df_clean[\'vaccines\'].apply(get_primary_vaccine)\n\nprint(f"\\nPrimary vaccine distribution:")\nprint(df_clean[\'primary_vaccine\'].value_counts())\n```\n```markdown\nNow I\'ll perform the One-Way ANOVA test with the properly filtered data.\n```\n```python\n# Group by primary vaccine and extract the values for ANOVA\ngroups = [group[\'total_vaccinations_per_hundred\'].tolist() \n          for name, group in df_clean.groupby(\'primary_vaccine\') if len(group) > 1]\n\n# Perform One-Way ANOVA\nf_statistic, p_value = stats.f_oneway(*groups)\n\nprint(f"ANOVA Results:")\nprint(f"F-statistic: {f_statistic:.4f}")\nprint(f"P-value: {p_value:.4f}")\nprint(f"\\nSignificant (p < 0.05): {p_value < 0.05}")\n```\n```markdown\n[STEP GOAL]: Verify the ANOVA results and prepare the final output in the required format.\n```\n```python\n# Round p-value to 4 decimal places\np_value_rounded = round(p_value, 4)\n\n# Determine significance\nsignificance = "yes" if p_value_rounded < 0.05 else "no"\n\nprint(f"Final Results:")\nprint(f"Significance: {significance}")\nprint(f"P-value: {p_value_rounded}")\n\n# Verify p-value is in valid range\nassert 0 <= p_value_rounded <= 1, "P-value must be between 0 and 1"\n```\nTime consumed: 0.01s\nCode output:\nExit Code: 0\nOutput: \nShape after filtering: (2090, 15)\n\nVaccine distribution after filtering:\nvaccines\nModerna, Oxford/AstraZeneca, Pfizer/BioNTech                                          820\nPfizer/BioNTech                                                                       348\nOxford/AstraZeneca, Pfizer/BioNTech                                                   332\nModerna, Pfizer/BioNTech                                                              241\nOxford/AstraZeneca                                                                     61\nPfizer/BioNTech, Sinovac                                                               58\nPfizer/BioNTech, Sinopharm/Beijing                                                     52\nOxford/AstraZeneca, Sinopharm/Beijing                                                  42\nOxford/AstraZeneca, Pfiz