sen.\n```\n```python\n# Check if Johnson&Johnson/Janssen exists in the original data\nprint("Johnson&Johnson/Janssen in original data:")\nprint(df[df[\'vaccines\'].str.contains(\'Johnson&Johnson/Janssen\', na=False)][\'vaccines\'].value_counts())\n\n# Check Moderna in original data\nprint("\\nModerna in original data:")\nprint(df[df[\'vaccines\'].str.contains(\'Moderna\', na=False)][\'vaccines\'].value_counts())\n```\n```markdown\nNow I need to properly assign each country to a single vaccine group. Since some countries use multiple vaccines, I\'ll use a priority order to assign the primary vaccine.\n```\n```python\n# Create a copy to avoid SettingWithCopyWarning\ndf_clean = df_filtered.dropna(subset=[\'total_vaccinations_per_hundred\']).copy()\n\n# Assign primary vaccine based on priority order\ndef get_primary_vaccine(vaccine_str):\n    if pd.isna(vaccine_str):\n        return None\n    # Priority order: Moderna, Oxford/AstraZeneca, Johnson&Johnson/Janssen, Pfizer/BioNTech\n    # This ensures Moderna gets priority over Pfizer when both are present\n    priority_order = [\'Moderna\', \'Oxford/AstraZeneca\', \'Johnson&Johnson/Janssen\', \'Pfizer/BioNTech\']\n    for vaccine in priority_order:\n        if vaccine in vaccine_str:\n            return vaccine\n    return None\n\ndf_clean[\'primary_vaccine\'] = df_clean[\'vaccines\'].apply(get_primary_vaccine)\n\nprint(f"Primary vaccine distribution:")\nprint(df_clean[\'primary_vaccine\'].value_counts())\n```\n```markdown\nNow I\'ll perform the One-Way ANOVA test with the properly filtered and grouped data.\n```\n```python\n# Group by primary vaccine and extract the values for ANOVA\n# Only include groups with more than 1 observation\ngroups = [group[\'total_vaccinations_per_hundred\'].tolist() \n          for name, group in df_clean.groupby(\'primary_vaccine\') if len(group) > 1]\n\n# Perform One-Way ANOVA\nf_statistic, p_value = stats.f_oneway(*groups)\n\nprint(f"ANOVA Results:")\nprint(f"F-statistic: {f_statistic:.4f}